//
// A CALL NO MEMBER TAKES IS REFUSED, NAMING THE ARGUMENT TYPES AND THE DECLARED DEFINITIONS
// (`@FR-Disp-Exhaustive`): for the STATIC types `(EPt, text)` no member of `ex` applies — a
// struct converts to neither `integer` nor `float`, and the second position needs an integer —
// so the call is refused at compile time; it never reaches a run-time "no applicable method".
// One refusal per file.
//
// @falsified-at: none — a conformance guard; the refusal fired as expected on 18594597b,
//   interpret and native.
// CHANNEL: the compile (the expected diagnostic).
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names the argument types and both members.
// HOLDS: a call each member takes (`ex(1, 2)`, `ex(1.5, 2)`) is not refused.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: no definition of `ex` takes (EPt, text) — declared: ex(integer, integer), ex(float, integer)

struct EPt { x: integer }

fn ex(a: integer, b: integer) -> text { "ii {a} {b}" }
fn ex(a: float, b: integer) -> text { "fi {a} {b}" }

fn main() {
  println("{ex(1, 2)}|{ex(1.5, 2)}");
  println(ex(EPt { x: 1 }, "t"));
}
